<title>UK: Wembley moves into H1 pre-tax profit.</title>
<headline>Wembley moves into H1 pre-tax profit.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-09-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Six months to June 30, 1996</p>
<p>			   (million stg unless stated)</p>
<p> Shr (pence)		10.0		   vs 26.7 loss</p>
<p> Pretax		     7.7		    vs 5.5 loss</p>
<p> Turnover		   64.85		  vs 52.38</p>
<p> Operating result	 15.8		   vs 9.2</p>
<p> Exceptional costs	nil		    vs 8.38</p>
<p> Interest payable	 2.88		   vs 6.42</p>
<p> Tax paid		   2.29		   vs 0.04</p>
<p> Net taxed		  5.37		   vs 5.54 loss</p>
<p>NOTE - Wembley Plc, the UK leisure venue group, said strong performances throughout the group had led to an increase in operating profit from continuing operations. Profits from its Wembley complex in west London were up 132.4 percent at 7.9 million stg. Track revenues in the U.S. were up 25 percent to 6.5 million stg, with continued improvement from video lottery terminals. Profits from UK greyhound tracks were unchanged at 1.4 million stg, despite the impact of the National Lottery, and profits from Keith Prowse Hospitality were boosted by 150 percent to 1.0 million stg, largely by the Euro '96 soccer tournament.</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom +44 171 542 7717</p>
